WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.149 --> 00:00:04.640
Almost 30 years ago, Back to the Future Part
II provided a glimpse of the future that seemed

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.420
funny and utterly fantastical at the time
— but has actually come true in a number

00:00:09.420 --> 00:00:12.849
of surprising ways.

00:00:12.849 --> 00:00:16.750
Here's how Back to the Future accurately predicted
current events.

00:00:16.750 --> 00:00:18.769
Movie magic

00:00:18.769 --> 00:00:22.590
While walking the streets of Hill Valley in
the future, Marty gets a taste of some futuristic

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:25.110
advertising at the local cinema.

00:00:25.140 --> 00:00:26.720
(screams)

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:33.610
While 3D movies in theaters have actually
been around since the 1950s, the trend didn't

00:00:33.610 --> 00:00:38.310
truly become a mainstream standard option
until James Cameron's Avatar wowed audiences

00:00:38.310 --> 00:00:39.730
in 2009.

00:00:39.730 --> 00:00:43.630
Since then, just about every new major release
has a 3D showing to go along with it.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:48.480
Of course, no matter how far we go with cinematic
magic, some folks will always be disappointed.

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:51.210
"The shark still looks fake."

00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.030
Can you see me now?

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:57.740
Older Marty McFly suffers the ultimate indignity
when his boss, Mr. Fujitsu, fires him while

00:00:57.740 --> 00:01:01.420
he's at home via video chat on his wall-mounted
video screen.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:03.080
"Read my fax!"

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:06.400
"No, please, I cannot be fired.

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.510
I'm fired!

00:01:07.510 --> 00:01:08.510
Ahhh…"

00:01:08.510 --> 00:01:09.720
That's not so crazy today!

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:14.400
Not only are flat, wall-hung TVs commonplace,
but employers can fire their underlings via

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:17.190
video all they like with software like Skype
and FaceTime.

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:21.710
But then, whatever points you give the movie
might be negated by its prediction of a house

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:23.430
filled with fax machines.

00:01:28.590 --> 00:01:30.479
Total tech necks

00:01:30.479 --> 00:01:34.530
When Marty's kids sit down to dinner wearing
tech-infused glasses, it seemed like a pretty

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:35.530
good gag.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:36.530
"Dad, telephone.

00:01:36.530 --> 00:01:37.620
It's Needles!"

00:01:37.620 --> 00:01:39.820
"Dad, it's for you."

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.890
Google Glass launched in 2013.

00:01:41.890 --> 00:01:45.610
And while smartglasses haven't quite broken
into the mainstream yet, gadget obsession

00:01:45.610 --> 00:01:46.990
certainly has.

00:01:46.990 --> 00:01:50.640
Everyone's had dinner with someone who can't
stop playing with their smartphones.

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:52.350
Don't be this guy.

00:01:52.350 --> 00:01:54.670
Robo reporters?

00:01:54.670 --> 00:01:59.460
When Griff and his gang of future flunkies
get arrested, a USA Today drone snaps a picture.

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:03.200
While the majority of today's journalists
and photographers are still actual human beings,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:04.960
this was still surprisingly accurate.

00:02:04.960 --> 00:02:08.899
These days, drones outfitted with cameras
are becoming pretty commonplace, and growing

00:02:08.899 --> 00:02:10.259
more so by the day.

00:02:10.259 --> 00:02:14.739
It's only a matter of time before E! News
has its own flying, robotic paparazzi to catch

00:02:14.739 --> 00:02:16.819
naked celebs.

00:02:16.819 --> 00:02:18.709
Garbage gas

00:02:18.709 --> 00:02:22.849
When Doc goes back to 1985 to bring Marty
and Jennifer to the future, he needs to refuel

00:02:22.849 --> 00:02:23.909
the DeLorean.

00:02:23.909 --> 00:02:27.799
But instead of heading to his friendly neighborhood
plutonium repository, he heads for the garbage

00:02:27.799 --> 00:02:28.799
can instead.

00:02:28.799 --> 00:02:29.799
"Wait a minute.

00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:30.799
What are you doing Doc?"

00:02:30.799 --> 00:02:31.799
"I need fuel."

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:34.680
"Go ahead, quick!

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.620
Get in the car."

00:02:36.629 --> 00:02:40.139
We're still pretty far off from using Mr.
Fusion in place of nuclear materials, but

00:02:40.139 --> 00:02:44.620
a method for using trash as fuel has actually
found its way into some countries.

00:02:44.620 --> 00:02:48.840
When organic matter — like leftover food
and biological waste — breaks down, it releases

00:02:48.840 --> 00:02:51.450
methane, carbon dioxide, and hydrogen.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:53.420
All of that can be combusted as fuel.

00:02:53.420 --> 00:02:57.170
These days, a few countries in Europe have
started adopting this technology.

00:02:57.170 --> 00:03:01.329
In fact, Sweden has had a biogas train since
2005.

00:03:01.329 --> 00:03:06.700
And in 2007, approximately 12,000 vehicles
used biogas fuel, most of them in Europe.

00:03:06.700 --> 00:03:11.590
It's only a matter of time until we're all
taking a more, um, active role in powering

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:16.230
our cars.

00:03:16.230 --> 00:03:17.230
Flying Fords

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:21.719
Invariably, whenever people take a look back
at yesterday's movies predicting tomorrow's

00:03:21.719 --> 00:03:24.979
technology, they always go back to one iconic
moment:

00:03:24.980 --> 00:03:25.740
"Roads?

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:29.770
Where we're going, we don't need… roads."

00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:34.620
"A flying DeLorean?"

00:03:39.189 --> 00:03:40.189
That's right.

00:03:40.189 --> 00:03:42.150
We're still not all driving flying cars.

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:44.169
But that doesn't mean we can't!

00:03:44.169 --> 00:03:48.919
Since the mid-2000s, a company called Terrafugia
has been working on making flying cars a reality.

00:03:48.919 --> 00:03:53.769
Sure, it actually looks more like a fold-up
plane than a car that can fly, but it's street-legal.

00:03:53.769 --> 00:03:56.339
All you have to do is figure out how to fly
the thing.

00:03:56.339 --> 00:03:59.870
And if that has you excited, the company has
plans for more flying vehicles to hit the

00:03:59.870 --> 00:04:02.579
roads and the skies in the next few years.

00:04:02.579 --> 00:04:07.059
Meanwhile, in September 2015, Toyota filed
a patent for a flying car of its own.

00:04:07.059 --> 00:04:10.829
Now that companies are competing to make flying
cars a reality, it's only a matter of time

00:04:10.829 --> 00:04:15.359
before we complain about rush hour from 30,000
feet.

00:04:15.359 --> 00:04:16.359
Hoverboards

00:04:16.359 --> 00:04:20.299
Of course, if people aren't complaining about
not having flying cars, that probably means

00:04:20.299 --> 00:04:22.550
they're just complaining about not having
this.

00:04:22.550 --> 00:04:27.130
The amazing — and entirely fictional — hoverboard
became an instant sensation as soon as it

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:31.229
appeared, and '80s and '90s kids have spent
decades demanding that science find a way

00:04:31.229 --> 00:04:32.900
to catch up to the fantasy.

00:04:32.900 --> 00:04:38.480
And while 2015 saw the popularization of dumb
devices called hoverboards that didn't actually,

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:40.960
y'know, hover, there's still hope.

00:04:40.960 --> 00:04:46.199
In August 2015, Lexus — yes, the car company
— picked up the torch and created a hoverboard

00:04:46.199 --> 00:04:47.770
that actually hovers.

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:52.060
The catch is that it needs a specially designed
track and won't be mass-produced, as it was

00:04:52.060 --> 00:04:53.840
only created for a commercial.

00:04:53.840 --> 00:04:57.639
Then there's the startup Arx Pax, which created
the Hendo Hoverboard.

00:04:57.639 --> 00:05:01.400
While videos of the ride show that the board
doesn't perform as well as the ones in the

00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:05.770
movie, it's enough for us to say that hoverboards
do, in fact exist.

00:05:05.770 --> 00:05:09.020
Just don't take it on the water.

00:05:09.020 --> 00:05:10.020
Cubs win!

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:15.370
Yes, in the '80s, the thought of the Cubs
actually breaking their decades-long World

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:19.919
Series drought was only slightly less absurd
than them actually doing so in the movie's

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:21.300
futuristic setting.

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:25.870
The gag about the Cubs finally taking the
title in 2015 got a lot of laughs, but it

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.430
very nearly came true.

00:05:27.430 --> 00:05:32.750
In 2016, the Cubs won the World Series in
dramatic fashion, taking Game 7 into extra

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:36.320
innings before squeaking out a one-run victory
over the Cleveland Indians.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.629
The fact that it all happened a year after
the movie predicted was noted by none other

00:05:40.629 --> 00:05:44.789
than Michael J. Fox, who added a couple of
sly callbacks when he congratulated the team

00:05:44.789 --> 00:05:46.370
via Twitter.

00:05:46.370 --> 00:05:48.470
President Trump

00:05:48.470 --> 00:05:52.560
When Back to the Future Part II was released,
businessman Donald Trump had accrued a certain

00:05:52.560 --> 00:05:56.729
measure of success, but he was mostly known
to people in the New York area.

00:05:56.729 --> 00:06:00.319
Most of them watched the movie without ever
suspecting that the future version of Biff,

00:06:00.319 --> 00:06:04.330
who uses his ill-gotten wealth to build a
casino empire that serves as a springboard

00:06:04.330 --> 00:06:07.020
into political power, was based on the mogul.

00:06:07.020 --> 00:06:11.310
In an interview with The Daily Beast in 2015,
the movie's writer, Bob Gale, explained his

00:06:11.310 --> 00:06:12.310
intentions:

00:06:12.310 --> 00:06:14.389
"We thought about it when we made the movie.

00:06:14.389 --> 00:06:17.550
There's a scene where Marty confronts Biff
in his office and there's a huge portrait

00:06:17.550 --> 00:06:22.031
of Biff on the wall behind Biff, and there's
one moment where Biff kind of stands up and

00:06:22.031 --> 00:06:24.710
he takes exactly the same pose as the portrait."

00:06:24.710 --> 00:06:27.620
Of course, Gale wasn't totally on point with
his prediction.

00:06:27.620 --> 00:06:30.680
Donald Trump has yet to make out with everybody's
mom.

00:06:30.680 --> 00:06:31.440
Probably.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.460
"Noooooo!!"

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:37.620
Thanks for watching!

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:40.210
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:40.210 --> 00:06:43.499
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

